1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
WebSockets and ServerSendEvents sound like two flavors of the same thing,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
but they're different territory, and mixing them up leads to a common, incorrect assumption about Clod.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This lesson maps the line between them, shows why Clod streaming isn't a WebSocket,

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
and covers the connection concerns you plan for on a live stream.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's break it down.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Let's define three terms up front, because the whole comparison rests on them.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
First, a WebSocket. That's a persistent, full-duplex connection.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Once it's open, both the client and the server can send messages at any time,

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
in either direction, over that same channel.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Second, ServerSendEvents. That's a one-way stream.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
The server pushes events to the client over a single HTTP response,

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
but the client can't send anything back on that same channel.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
And third, the word full-duplex itself.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
It just means traffic flowing both directions at once,

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
like a phone call where both people can talk simultaneously.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
A one-way channel, by contrast, only carries traffic in a single direction.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Full-duplex WebSocket, one-way ServerSendEvents.

18
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
That contrast is the heart of the lesson.

19
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Let's put the two side-by-side and see where each shines.

20
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A WebSocket is bidirectional and persistent.

21
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
It's built for applications where both sides push messages constantly.

22
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Think live chat, collaborative editing, or a multiplayer game.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The tradeoff is more setup.

24
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
A WebSocket has its own protocol and an opening handshake to establish the connection.

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
ServerSendEvents are simpler.

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Traffic flows one direction only, from server to client,

27
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
and it rides over plain HTTP.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
There's no special handshake,

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
and because it looks like an ordinary HTTP response,

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
it tends to pass through firewalls and proxies without trouble.

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Here's the punchline for builders.

32
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The Cloud API uses ServerSendEvents for streaming, not WebSockets.

33
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
So when you stream from Cloud, you're on the simpler one-way path.

34
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Now let's kill the myth directly,

35
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
because it's the most common misconception in this area.

36
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
People assume that because Cloud streams tokens in real time,

37
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
there has to be a WebSocket behind it. There isn't.

38
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Cloud's token streaming uses ServerSendEvents. Full stop.

39
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
You never open a WebSocket to talk to the Cloud API.

40
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Every call you make, whether you're streaming or not,

41
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
is a plain HTTPS request to the same endpoint.

42
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Streaming just changes how the response comes back,

43
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
not the kind of connection you open.

44
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Why does this matter?

45
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Because if you sketch an architecture that assumes a two-way socket to Cloud,

46
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
that design rests on a false premise.

47
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
The channel to Cloud is request and response over HTTP,

48
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
with streaming layered on as one-way events.

49
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
So if Cloud doesn't use them,

50
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
when are WebSockets actually the right tool?

51
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
It's worth knowing, because the pattern is genuinely useful elsewhere.

52
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
WebSockets shine when your app needs constant, low-latency traffic

53
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
in both directions at once.

54
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Live collaboration, where several people edit one document

55
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
and every keystroke syncs.

56
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Multiplayer games, where the server and every client push state continuously.

57
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
A chat server that both sends and receives in real time.

58
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
In all of those, a persistent two-way channel earns its keep.

59
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
But reaching Cloud is a different shape of problem.

60
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
You send a request, you get a response,

61
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and if you want it incrementally,

62
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
you turn on streaming and read server-sent events.

63
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
There's no scenario in the Cloud API where you'd open a WebSocket.

64
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Right tool, wrong place.

65
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Let's shift to something practical.

66
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Once you're working with a long-lived streaming connection,

67
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
you have to plan for it dropping, because eventually one will.

68
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Start with timeouts.

69
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
The SDK sets a generous request timeout, around 10 minutes by default.

70
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
That sounds like plenty, but a big generation can genuinely run long.

71
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Here's a related detail.

72
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
If you ask for a very large response without streaming,

73
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
the SDK may refuse the call,

74
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
because it estimates the blocking request would blow past that timeout.

75
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The fix is to stream it, which keeps data flowing and the connection alive.

76
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And when a connection drops midstream,

77
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
you don't get a clean, complete response.

78
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
You get, whatever arrived before the break, a partial message.

79
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
So treat the stream as something that can fail,

80
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
and don't assume it always runs to completion.

81
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Let's name three concerns every builder plans for on a streaming connection.

82
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
First, timeouts.

83
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
That's the ceiling on how long a request can run before the client gives up.

84
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Large streamed responses can push toward that ceiling,

85
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
which is exactly why streaming keeps the data moving.

86
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Second, retries with backoff.

87
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
When a request fails for a temporary reason,

88
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
the client resends it, waiting a little longer between each attempt.

89
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
The SDK does this automatically for transient errors,

90
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
a couple of times by default.

91
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Third, ping and error events.

92
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Your stream can carry more than content.

93
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
A ping is just a keep alive, you can ignore,

94
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
and an error event signals that something went wrong.

95
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Timeouts, retries, and the odd interleaved event.

96
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Plan for all three, and your streaming code stays robust.

97
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Let's zoom in on those interleaved events,

98
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
because a naive reader trips over them.

99
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Your stream is mostly content events, but not only content events.

100
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Every so often you'll get a ping.

101
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
A ping is a keep alive with no real payload.

102
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Its only job is keeping the connection from going idle,

103
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
so you ignore it and move on.

104
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
You'll also sometimes get an error event,

105
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
and this one you can't ignore.

106
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
An error can arrive right in the middle of a stream,

107
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and it needs real handling.

108
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Logging it, surfacing it, maybe retrying the request.

109
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
And here's a defensive habit.

110
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
If you see an event type you don't recognize,

111
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
skip it rather than crashing,

112
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
since new types can be added over time.

113
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
So read defensively.

114
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Ignore pings, handle errors, skip anything unexpected,

115
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
and one stray event won't take down your loop.

116
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
This brings up a distinction that saves real debugging time.

117
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Not every failure deserves a retry.

118
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Split your errors into two buckets.

119
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
The first is transient errors.

120
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Timeouts, rate limits, network blips, and 500 series server errors.

121
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
These are expected to clear on their own,

122
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
so the right move is to retry,

123
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
ideally with exponential backoff,

124
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
waiting a bit longer each attempt.

125
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
The SDK already does this on the errors it recognizes.

126
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
The second bucket is permanent errors.

127
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
A bad request, a malformed schema, an auth failure.

128
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Retrying these is pointless, because the input itself is wrong.

129
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Sending the same broken request again

130
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
just reproduces the same failure and burns budget.

131
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
So classify first, then act.

132
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
If it's transient, retry with backoff.

133
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
If it's permanent, fix the request.

134
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Matching the response to the type is the whole skill.

135
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Here's the bottom line.

136
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:26,000
WebSockets and server-sent events are different animals.

137
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
A WebSocket is a persistent two-way channel

138
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
for constant back-and-forth.

139
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Claude's streaming is a one-way server-sent event

140
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
over plain HTTPS.

141
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Never a WebSocket.

142
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:41,000
So plan for dropped streams, retry transient errors,

143
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
and ignore pings while handling real errors.

144
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
That wraps up WebSockets versus SSE for Claude.

145
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
Catch you in the next one.

